 wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$(n+2)^{2}\\le {3}^{n+1}$ for all $n\\ge 1$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements." 13194139534203 17592186045417 true] [17592186413945 1072 #uuid "26d461a4-4d45-54f1-9397-8e13a8aa54ef" 13194139534203 17592186045417 true] [17592186413945 1074 "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n(n+2)^{2}\\le {3}^{n+1}\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called **base case**, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n(1+2)^{2}\\le {3}^{1+1}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n(k+2)^{2}\\le {3}^{k+1}\n$$\n\nThen, we move on to the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((k+1)+2)^{2} & \\le {3}^{(k+1)+1} \\\\ (k+3)^{2} & \\le {3}^{k+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives:\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n(k+3)^{2}\\le {3}^{k+2}.\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this inequality using the inductive hypothesis and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is less than or equal to the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, $(n+2)^{2}\\le {3}^{n+1}$ for all $n\\ge 1.$" 13194139534203 17592186045417 true] [17592186413946 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413930 13194139534203 17592186045417 true] [17592186413946 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413932 13194139534203 17592186045417 true] [17592186413946 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413933 13194139534203 17592186045417 true] [17592186413946 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413935 13194139534203 17592186045417 true] [17592186413946 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413940 13194139534203 17592186045417 true] [17592186413946 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413942 13194139534203 17592186045417 true] [17592186413946 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413943 13194139534203 17592186045417 true] [17592186413946 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413944 13194139534203 17592186045417 true] [17592186413946 1069 "q-183370" 13194139534203 17592186045417 true] [17592186413946 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534203 17592186045417 true] [17592186413946 1071 "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n${3}^{n}>1+{2}^{n}$ for all $n\\ge 2$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements." 13194139534203 17592186045417 true] [17592186413946 1072 #uuid "f75ec7c6-91c3-5a79-95d1-5c3dc1cb4b13" 13194139534203 17592186045417 true] [17592186413946 1074 "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{3}^{n}>1+{2}^{n}\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called **base case**. Since we are required to prove the statement for $n\\ge 2,$ the base case is $P(2).$ So, we substitute $n=2$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=2.$\n\n$$\n{3}^{2}>1+{2}^{2}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show that the inequality holds.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 2.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{3}^{k}>1+{2}^{k}\n$$\n\nThen, we move on to the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{3}^{k+1}>1+{2}^{k+1}.\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this inequality using the inductive hypothesis and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is greater than the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${3}^{n}>1+{2}^{n}$ for all $n\\ge 2.$" 13194139534203 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-086469b0-50ff-592d-907c-51a0b13d0499" 17592186413872 "answer-13ac22e4-0199-5c66-ae33-88e24383ed14" 17592186413873 "answer-14ed47d8-08f8-50bd-9688-8584885905ba" 17592186413874 "answer-16d38fae-3ad8-5767-b6cc-27f8394c79cc" 17592186413875 "answer-17b84aae-4020-5754-93c5-377d88248110" 17592186413876 "answer-25eeffa7-a41d-5c66-8ad0-584b2b71a043" 17592186413877 "answer-2de08096-ec85-5a9d-b85b-72829e4c3b75" 17592186413878 "answer-312a36e5-3779-5d34-9711-3861a088a559" 17592186413879 "answer-327b0dc1-be5e-5a59-aac9-4f8c97c53bdf" 17592186413880 "answer-3d2077da-26c1-555b-9de1-664d038f0985" 17592186413881 "answer-3d5bfae7-2182-5979-b11f-d229d95be319" 17592186413882 "answer-46298bba-b70b-5a69-8da6-e54218c2f861" 17592186413883 "answer-4e640d6a-beb2-57b7-a9dd-f43